<p>English economist William Stanley Jevons introduced a striking paper in 1875: <a href="https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.228276/page/n225/mode/2up">&#8220;On the Influence of the Sun-Spot Period Upon the Price of Corn.&#8221;</a> He said his statistical studies had suggested that business cycles vary with sunspot activity:</p>
<blockquote><p>
The success of the harvest in any year certainly depends upon the weather, especially that of the summer and autumn months. Now, if this weather depends in any degree upon the solar period, it follows that the harvest and the price of grain will depend more or less upon the solar period, and will go through periodic fluctuations in periods of time equal to those of the sun-spots.
</p></blockquote>
<p>The idea wasn&#8217;t borne out, but a 2001 study has found <a href="https://scholarsarchive.byu.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=10180&#038;context=facpub">that sunny weather has a small impact on stock returns</a> &#8212; probably because it improves traders&#8217; moods.</p>
